[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is router2’s firewall (iptables) in the forwarding path, not the RIP daemon. Traffic from the IT (192.168.1.0/24) and CS (192.168.2.0/24) VLANs reaches router2 but is dropped when being forwarded to the Server Room (1.0.0.0/8) and Internet Lab (128.168.0.0/16). This matches the symptom that Other (192.168.3.0/24) and Principle (192.168.4.0/24) users work while IT/CS users do not.

Mechanism: traceroutes from IT/CS hosts reach hop 3 (20.20.0.2 = router2) and then star out, consistent with a silent forward drop. IT/CS hosts can ping router2’s local interfaces (1.0.0.1, 128.168.0.1), proving L3 to router2 is fine and the drop occurs only on transit traffic. On router2, iptables shows explicit blocks in FORWARD: “-A FORWARD -s 192.168.1.0/24 -j DROP” and “-A FORWARD -s 192.168.2.0/24 -j DROP,” while INPUT policy is ACCEPT. Meanwhile, RIP is healthy: router2’s “show ip rip” lists learned routes (e.g., R(n) 10.0.0.0/8 via 20.20.0.1, R(n) 192.168.{1,2,3,4}.0/24), and all routers’ routing tables contain the expected RIP routes. Router0 can ping 1.0.0.5 and 128.168.0.2 because its source (10.10.0.1) is not filtered, further isolating the issue to router2’s FORWARD rules targeting only 192.168.1.0/24 and 192.168.2.0/24.

Root cause: Two unintended iptables FORWARD drop rules were added on router2 during maintenance, blocking IT/CS-sourced transit traffic.

Corrective fix: On router2, remove or adjust the offending rules (e.g., iptables -D FORWARD -s 192.168.1.0/24 -j DROP; iptables -D FORWARD -s 192.168.2.0/24 -j DROP), then persist the corrected firewall configuration. No RIP restart is required. 
=== END ===